  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on decadal interval satellite observations, which may overlook short-term seasonal variations or rapid transitional phases in land surface temperature dynamics.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the spatial changes in land use, land cover, and land surface temperature in Bhopal, India, over a 20-year period (2000, 2010, and 2020) using Landsat 5-TM and Landsat 8-OLI satellite imagery. By calculating six remote sensing indices, including NDVI, NDBI, and NDWI, the research quantified how the expansion of built-up areas and bare soil, alongside the loss of vegetation and water bodies, correlated with rising urban temperatures.",
      "why": "It provides empirical, long-term spatial evidence of how specific land cover transitions drive local temperature increases, offering urban planners concrete localized data to support climate-resilient zoning and green infrastructure policies."
    }
